    "Initial Spearman analysis found a negative association between discount percentage\n",
    "and profit (ρ = -0.2035, p < 0.001).\n",
    "\n",
    "Because discount percentage was also positively associated with quantity purchased\n",
    "(ρ = 0.2871, p < 0.001), quantity was considered as a potential confounding factor.\n",
    "Descriptive analysis showed that larger-quantity purchases tended to receive larger\n",
    "discounts, meaning the observed relationship between discounts and quantity should\n",
    "not be interpreted causally.\n",
    "\n",
    "A multiple linear regression was therefore used to examine the relationship between\n",
    "discount percentage and profit while accounting for quantity, unit price, and product\n",
    "cost.\n",
    "\n",
    "The model explained 68.9% of the variation in item-level profit (R² = 0.689).\n",
    "After controlling for the other variables, discount percentage remained negatively\n",
    "associated with profit (β = -7.46, p < 0.001, 95% CI [-7.50, -7.42]).\n",
    "\n",
    "### Business Interpretation\n",
    "\n",
    "Within this dataset, each additional percentage point of discount is associated with\n",
    "approximately $7.46 lower item-level profit, holding quantity, unit price, and product\n",
    "cost constant.\n",
    "\n",
    "Higher-discount transactions also tend to involve larger quantities, so the data do\n",
    "not establish that discounts cause customers to purchase more. Despite the larger\n",
    "quantities observed at higher discount levels, profitability generally declines as\n",
    "discounts increase.\n",
    "\n",
    "Items receiving discounts above 50% generated a negative average profit in the\n",
    "observed data, suggesting that very high discount levels warrant particular scrutiny.\n",
    "\n",
    "These findings describe associations in the available observational data and should\n",
    "not be interpreted as causal effects of discounting."
